# Blackmagic-style 4K cinema camera + 35 mm T2.1 cine prime
#
# THE CAMERA IN THIS SCENE IS THE CAMERA YOU ARE LOOKING THROUGH. The GLB's own
# optical axis and front-element vertex were solved from its geometry (tools/
# camera_rig.py), and the mesh is placed so that vertex sits exactly on the render
# camera's entrance pupil, pointing down +Z. So the body, the barrel and the finder
# are all BEHIND the eye and can never appear in frame -- but they are still solid,
# so the camera occludes the key light and casts its own shadow into its own
# photograph. That shadow across the near bench is the proof the rig is real and not
# a free-floating viewpoint.
#
# The asset labels its own lens: '35  T2.1' and 'FILTER 88MM' are painted on
# the barrel, and the body reads '4K'. That is a Blackmagic Production Camera 4K
# with a 35 mm cine prime. The two labels give two INDEPENDENT scale anchors and
# they agree to 1%: the solved barrel rim is 0.5416 model units in radius, so an
# 88 mm filter thread means 81.24 mm per unit, and at that scale the 2.0-unit body
# comes out 162.5 mm against the real camera's 161 mm.
#
# T2.1 is a TRANSMISSION stop -- the geometric aperture behind it is about f/2.0
# once the ~10% loss through a cine prime's elements is taken out, and f/2.0 is
# what the ray tracer needs. Sensor is the BMPC4K's 21.12 x 11.88 mm Super-35
# (native 16:9, hence the 1280x720 film).
#
# This GLB ships a 17.5-unit ground plane bundled in with the camera, on material
# 'ground'. `skip_material ground` drops it at load; without that it would cut
# straight through the bench.
#
# --- solved placement (do not hand-edit; regenerate with tools/camera_rig.py) ----
#   model bbox    [2.     1.6451 3.455 ]  (162.5 mm wide -> scale 0.08125)
#   optical axis  [6.96000e-04 8.62000e-04 9.99999e-01]   (residual after the rotation below: 0.0000 deg)
#   front vertex  [-1.89000e-03 -1.04400e-02  2.26719e+00]  -> world origin
#   front rim     88.0 mm across
#   the body sits 63.1 mm below its own axis, so the bench top is at y = -0.0631
#     and the camera is RESTING on it (eye height above the bench = its real one)
#
# --- what this variant is --------------------------------------------------------
# ANALYTIC rig -- mode A, the finite-lens forward physical camera. The aperture is a
# real disc of radius focal/2N (8.75 mm here) and photons are splatted through it,
# so the field of view and the depth of field are exactly right. What it does NOT have
# is glass: the lens is an ideal thin element, so there is no spherical or chromatic
# aberration, no distortion, no field curvature, and the blur discs are perfectly
# uniform right into the corners.
#
# So this is the CONTROL. On this render the star row should be a row of clean points
# all the way to the frame edge, and the bokeh row should be a row of identical round
# discs including the outermost one. Compare against camrig_cinema_sim.ftsl, which is
# the same camera -- same focal length, same f-number, same sensor, same subject --
# with the ideal element replaced by traced glass; every difference between the two
# images is exactly what the glass did.
#
#   ftrace -in scenes/camrig_cinema.ftsl -camera taking -time 900 \
#          -window -keepwindow -interval 20  -checkpoint -o png/camrigs/cinema.png
#
# The scene carries a second camera, "witness", an outside 3/4 view of the whole rig
# so you can see the camera model that this photograph is being taken THROUGH:
#
#   ftrace -in scenes/camrig_cinema.ftsl -camera witness -mode R -time 120 \
#          -window -keepwindow -o png/camrigs/cinema_witness.png

scene { units meters  spectral 360 830 1 }

# --- the bench ------------------------------------------------------------------
# A checkered top, 45 mm squares, running away from the camera and meeting the
# backdrop at z = 2.60. It is the continuous depth reference: the square size is
# fixed, so how fast the squares shrink IS the focal length, and where they stop
# resolving IS the depth of field. The camera is RESTING on this bench -- its own
# base sits 63.1 mm below its own optical axis, which is why the top is at
# y = -0.0631 and not some round number.
pattern  "chk"      { type checker  size 0.045 }
material "chk_pale" { type diffuse reflect rgb 0.74 0.71 0.66 }
material "chk_dark" { type diffuse reflect rgb 0.11 0.11 0.13 }
material "bench"    { type mix  layer "chk_pale" 0.5  layer "chk_dark" 0.5  weight_map pattern:chk }
material "backdrop" { type diffuse reflect rgb 0.44 0.45 0.48 }
quad { origin -2.20 -0.0631 -1.00   u 4.40 0 0   v 0 0 3.60   material bench }
quad { origin -2.60 -0.0631 2.60    u 5.20 0 0   v 0 2.40 0   material backdrop }

# --- the resolution charts -------------------------------------------------------
# Three upright 120 x 140 mm cards on a fine 10 mm checker, standing on the bench and
# facing the lens. The pale one at z = 1.00 m is the focus target; the other two are
# 0.45 m and 0.95 m behind it, so how fast their checker mushes IS the depth of field.
# Their lateral positions are solved so the three angular spans are disjoint from each
# other and from the star row, hence the odd x values -- with the eye at bench height,
# an object LYING on the bench reads as a smear on the horizon, so the subject stands.
pattern "chkfine" { type checker  size 0.010 }
material "card_dark" { type diffuse reflect rgb 0.05 0.05 0.06 }
material "card_pale0" { type diffuse reflect rgb 0.86 0.84 0.8 }
material "card0" { type mix  layer "card_pale0" 0.5  layer "card_dark" 0.5  weight_map pattern:chkfine }
material "card_pale1" { type diffuse reflect rgb 0.3 0.5 0.62 }
material "card1" { type mix  layer "card_pale1" 0.5  layer "card_dark" 0.5  weight_map pattern:chkfine }
material "card_pale2" { type diffuse reflect rgb 0.62 0.44 0.26 }
material "card2" { type mix  layer "card_pale2" 0.5  layer "card_dark" 0.5  weight_map pattern:chkfine }
quad { origin 0.0400 -0.0631 1.000   u 0 0.140 0   v 0.120 0 0   material card0 }
quad { origin -0.1870 -0.0631 1.450   u 0 0.140 0   v 0.120 0 0   material card1 }
quad { origin 0.4000 -0.0631 1.950   u 0 0.140 0   v 0.120 0 0   material card2 }

# --- the star row (in focus): the point-spread-function readout ------------------
# 3 mm pinpoints floating AT the focus distance and exactly ON the optical axis plane,
# 63 mm over the bench, fanned sideways across the whole frame. An in-focus
# point source images to the lens's PSF, so this row is a star test: the analytic rig's
# ideal element collapses every one of them to a few pixels no matter how far off axis,
# while traced glass flares the outer ones into coma and fringes them with lateral
# colour. y = 0 makes the centre star the true ON-AXIS reference and displaces the
# rest purely laterally, so the row is a clean sweep of field angle; it also puts them
# above the bench/backdrop horizon, read against flat grey instead of bench glare.
# Count the ones in frame and you have read the field of view directly.
light sphere { center 0.000 0.000 1.000  radius 0.0015  spd blackbody 5500 }
light sphere { center 0.190 0.000 1.000  radius 0.0015  spd blackbody 5500 }
light sphere { center -0.190 0.000 1.000  radius 0.0015  spd blackbody 5500 }
light sphere { center 0.380 0.000 1.000  radius 0.0015  spd blackbody 5500 }
light sphere { center -0.380 0.000 1.000  radius 0.0015  spd blackbody 5500 }
light sphere { center 0.570 0.000 1.000  radius 0.0015  spd blackbody 5500 }
light sphere { center -0.570 0.000 1.000  radius 0.0015  spd blackbody 5500 }
light sphere { center 0.780 0.000 1.000  radius 0.0015  spd blackbody 5500 }
light sphere { center -0.780 0.000 1.000  radius 0.0015  spd blackbody 5500 }

# --- the bokeh row (1.32 m behind focus): the aperture readout -------------------
# The same pinpoints, warm and 6 mm across, far enough back to be thoroughly defocused.
# Blur-disc diameter on the sensor is A*f*(u-uf)/(u*(uf-f)), pure entrance pupil:
# ~5 px at 30 mm f/3.5, ~22 px at 35 mm f/2.0. Under the simulated rig the outer
# ones additionally go cat's-eye, clipped at both ends of the barrel. They sit high
# enough (y = 0.32) that no resolution card can eclipse one on any camera in the set.
light sphere { center 0.000 0.320 2.320  radius 0.0030  spd blackbody 2700 }
light sphere { center 0.190 0.320 2.320  radius 0.0030  spd blackbody 2700 }
light sphere { center -0.190 0.320 2.320  radius 0.0030  spd blackbody 2700 }
light sphere { center 0.400 0.320 2.320  radius 0.0030  spd blackbody 2700 }
light sphere { center -0.400 0.320 2.320  radius 0.0030  spd blackbody 2700 }
light sphere { center 0.620 0.320 2.320  radius 0.0030  spd blackbody 2700 }
light sphere { center -0.620 0.320 2.320  radius 0.0030  spd blackbody 2700 }
light sphere { center 0.860 0.320 2.320  radius 0.0030  spd blackbody 2700 }
light sphere { center -0.860 0.320 2.320  radius 0.0030  spd blackbody 2700 }

# --- lighting -------------------------------------------------------------------
# BOTH lights sit BEHIND the eye (z < 0), which is forced by two independent things:
#
#   * the resolution cards face -Z, i.e. they face the lens. A light in front of them
#     lights their BACKS, and all three would render as flat silhouettes lit only by
#     bounce. Anything meant to light the subject has to be behind the camera.
#   * the camera's OWN SHADOW is the whole point of the rig -- the body is behind the
#     eye and can never be in frame, but it is still solid. A key in FRONT throws that
#     shadow backwards, behind the camera, where no one can see it. Only a key behind
#     and above throws it FORWARD, across the near bench and into the frame.
#
# The key is placed by solving that shadow: the ray from the key over the body's top
# edge meets the bench about 0.5 m in front of the lens and ~13 deg off axis, inside
# even the narrowest frame here. Being behind the eye, neither light can be stared at
# directly by the taking camera, and both are outside the witness camera's frame too.
light sphere { center -0.25 0.35 -1.10  radius 0.130  spd blackbody 5200 }
light sphere { center  0.85 0.55 -0.20  radius 0.100  spd blackbody 4200 }

# --- the camera itself -----------------------------------------------------------
# The GLB carries its own pbrMetallicRoughness materials, so the FTSL `material` line
# is only a FALLBACK for primitives that have none (it would take over entirely under
# `import_materials no`).
material "camfallback" { type diffuse  reflect rgb 0.38 0.38 0.42 }
mesh "body" {
    file "cameras/cinema_camera.glb"
    material camfallback
    skip_material ground        # this asset bundles a backdrop plane
    scale 0.08125
    rotate 0.04937 -0.03989 0.00000
    translate 0.000282 0.001007 -0.184208
}

camera "taking" {
    eye 0 0 0   look_at 0 0 1   up 0 1 0
    mode A
    lens 35   fstop 2   focus 1.000
    film { res 1280 720  size 21.12 11.88 }
}

# An outside view of the rig: the camera model on the bench, with the still life it
# is photographing behind it. Pinhole, so nothing here is a claim about the optics.
camera "witness" {
    eye -0.80 0.40 -0.62   look_at 0.02 -0.0231 0.58   up 0 1 0
    fov_y 42   mode R
    film { res 1200 800 }
}
